# Built-in Shomate coefficients for common gases.
#
# Coefficients give Cp in J/(mol·K) with t = T(K)/1000. Source is the NIST
# Chemistry WebBook unless an entry says otherwise. Valid ranges differ per
# entry: read each entry's `t_min`/`t_max`.
#
# Species that need several fits to cover their range (N₂, O₂, CO₂, H₂O)
# appear once per temperature segment under the same formula string. The
# segments of one species are adjacent, in increasing temperature order,
# and contiguous (each `t_max` equals the next segment's `t_min`). Use
# [`lookup_shomate_at`] to select the segment for a temperature;
# [`lookup_shomate`] returns the lowest-temperature segment.
#
# Sections of the Rust literal:
# - rows 25–33 (C3H8(g) 298–1200 K … Ne(g) 298–6000 K): Additional Shomate data
#
# Notes on rows, from the Rust literal:
# - row 0, H2O(g) 298–1700 K: NIST WebBook gives no gas-phase set below 500 K. Its 500–1700 K
#   set is used down to 298 K as a documented extrapolation so flame calculations can start at
#   298.15 K: Cp(298.15) = 33.59 and Cp(400) = 34.26 J/(mol·K), matching JANAF (33.590, 34.262)
#   to < 0.1 %.
# - row 1, H2O(g) 1700–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 1700–6000 K.
# - row 2, CO2(g) 298–1200 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 298–1200 K.
# - row 3, CO2(g) 1200–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 1200–6000 K.
# - row 4, N2(g) 100–500 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 100–500 K.
# - row 5, N2(g) 500–2000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 500–2000 K.
# - row 6, N2(g) 2000–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 2000–6000 K.
# - row 7, O2(g) 100–700 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 100–700 K.
# - row 8, O2(g) 700–2000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 700–2000 K.
# - row 9, O2(g) 2000–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 2000–6000 K.
# - row 11, CH4(g) 1300–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 1300–6000 K
# - row 13, CO(g) 1300–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 1300–6000 K
# - row 15, H2(g) 1000–2500 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 1000–2500 K
# - row 16, H2(g) 2500–6000 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 2500–6000 K
# - row 22, C2H6(g) 298–1200 K: Approximate: not a NIST WebBook Shomate set (NIST publishes none
#   for ethane) and the source is unknown. Known error: Cp(298.15) = 54.3 vs reference 52.5
#   J/(mol·K) (+3.4 %). Use with caution.
# - row 23, C2H4(g) 298–1200 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 298–1200 K (Cp(298.15) = 42.85 J/(mol·K)).
# - row 25, C3H8(g) 298–1200 K: Approximate: not a NIST WebBook Shomate set (NIST publishes none
#   for propane) and the source is unknown. Known error: Cp(298.15) = 79.8 vs reference 73.6
#   J/(mol·K) (+8.4 %). Use with caution.
# - row 28, Br2(g) 332.5–3400 K: NIST WebBook Shomate, 332.5–3400 K.
# - row 29, HF(g) 298–1200 K: KNOWN DEFECT — not the NIST set and of unknown source. This fit
#   gives Cp(298.15) = 28.77 J/(mol·K) (JANAF: 29.14), falls to ~27.8 near 700 K, and stays
#   below the 7R/2 = 29.10 floor of a diatomic gas across its whole range, which is physically
#   impossible. Do not rely on HF heat capacities until this is replaced with a sourced fit.
# Rust: kimiya::thermochem::SHOMATE_DATA (its length).
